Case 1: One negative and two positives.
Possible combinations:
- (-1) × 1 × 6 = -6 ⇒ triplet {-1, 1, 6}
- (-1) × 2 × 3 = -6 ⇒ triplet {-1, 2, 3}
- (-2) × 1 × 3 = -6 ⇒ triplet {-2, 1, 3}
- (-3) × 1 × 2 = -6 ⇒ triplet {-3, 1, 2}
- (-1) × -1 × 6 → not distinct
- (-1) × -2 × 3 → product is 6, not -6
So only the four triplets listed above.

Case 2: Three negatives. Product of three negatives is negative. Let's see:
- (-1) × (-2) × (-3) = -6 ⇒ triplet {-1, -2, -3}
Check product: (-1)(-2)(-3) = -6. Yes, this works. So this is another triplet.
